  "Number": 39,
  "Question": "If \\(y=x^2+px+q,\\) then if the least possible value of \\(y\\) is zero, \\(q\\) is equal to:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(0\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac{p^2}{4}\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\dfrac p2\\)",
    "D": "\\(-\\dfrac p2\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac{p^2}{4}-q\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "For real \\(x\\), completing the square separates a nonnegative term from a constant:\n\\[\ny=x^2+px+q\n=\\left(x+\\frac p2\\right)^2+q-\\frac{p^2}{4}.\n\\]\nThe square is always nonnegative and equals zero at \\(x=-p/2\\). Hence the least possible value of \\(y\\) is exactly \\(q-p^2/4\\). The given minimum is zero, so\n\\[\nq-\\frac{p^2}{4}=0,\n\\qquad \\boxed{q=\\frac{p^2}{4}}.\n\\]\nThis is choice B.",
